      "text": "Since $-2$ must be in the range of  $f(x)=x^2+3x+c$, we must have:  $$-2=x^2+3x+c$$\n\nHence: $$x^2+3x+c+2=0.$$ The above quadratic must necessarily have real solutions. Because of this, its discriminant must be non-negative. So we now take the discriminant: $$1^2-4x(-2+2)\\stackrel{?}{\\geq}0$$ $$1\\geq0$$ $1\\geq0$ is true for all values of $x$. But we must maximaze $c$ to get the largest value of $c$.\nTo maximize $c$, we need to find the $x$ that maximizes the right side of our equation: $$1+x-4(x-2)\\stackrel{?}{\\geq}c$$ $$1+c+8\\stackrel{?}{\\geq}c$$ $$-7\\stackrel{?}{\\geq}0$$ $-7\\stackrel{?}{\\geq}0$ is false for all values of $x$, so our largest value for $c$ is 4. But if $c$ is even smaller, say zero, then the $x$ in our equation will have negative $x-2$ and $-2$ won't be in the range of polynomials with negative leading coefficients. Thus, our largest value of $x$ is 2, so our largest value of $c$ is $\\boxed{4}$.\nThere are many mistakes made in the solution, but we will solve them one at a time.\n\n2. The first part of the solution misses the crucial step of saying that $-2$ must be in the range of $f(x)$ for it to be a solution.\n\n3. The second part of the solution is incorrect because $\\geq$ can only be true for all real values of $x$, and not necessarily for the $x$ that maximize the right side. It is specifically the ones that maximize the right side we are concerned about. Also, this makes the intention of the author to get the maximum value came out just as a sweet unnecessary comment and small added lucky assumption.\n\nReading the problem carefully leads to us getting the wrong equation to solve. When the problem says $-2$ is a value in the range of the function it doesn't mean it's the only value. It means $-2$ can be any function of $y$. At first glance, it is the only thing that was mentioned, but on second glance the problem is asking about all function where $-2$ is in the range of that function. \n\nTo find all these functions, we note the nature of quadratic equations. A quartic is defined with the coefficients $c,x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4$ in its form $ax^4+bx^3+cx^2+dx+e$. Barring trivial cases, the discriminant for a quadratic equation $ax^2+bx+c=0$ is $b^2-4ac$ and is needed here as well. So we see the quartics for $-2$ in the range of $f(x)=x^2+3x+c$ is:\n\n1. $x^2+3x-c^2\\ge -2$ and $x^2+3x-c^2+4<0$ for some $c\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n2. $x^2+3x-c^2\\ge -2$ and $x^2+3x-c^2+4>0$ for some $c\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n3. $x^2+3x-c^2\\ge -2$ and $x^2+3x-c^2+4=0$ for some $c\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n4. $x^2+3x-c^2=-2$ and $x^2+3x-c^2+4=0$ for some $c\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nWe then check $\\boxed{c=4}$ as it is made possible by (3), (4), and (1) as shown in the detailed solution provided. Setting $c=4$, we see $-2=4, 1, 2$ are the only possible functions as shown in the solution.\nThis problem was pretty common trend for the last 5 years on Mathcounts math competitions. I finally realized that also, actually for CMPC And P2 Annual. I wish I found the solution in time, because I got the oopoortuies of one full round of CMC P2 and PAMV!\nThis was an easy problem. Solving this problem requires only $5$ steps. In order to make the problem easy, I had to think about it before posting it.",
