      "text": "To find the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of the quadratic function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to ensure that there is some \\( x \\) such that \\( f(x) = -2 \\). This gives us the equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c = -2\n\\]\n\nRearranging this equation to standard quadratic form, we get:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nFor \\(-2\\) to be in the range of the function, the quadratic equation \\(x^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0\\) must have real roots. A quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) has real roots if and only if its discriminant is non-negative. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of the quadratic equation \\(x^2 + 3x + (c + 2) = 0\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 3\\), and \\(c = c + 2\\). Substituting these values into the discriminant formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (c + 2) = 9 - 4(c + 2) = 9 - 4c - 8 = 1 - 4c\n\\]\n\nFor the quadratic to have real roots, the discriminant must be non-negative:\n\n\\[\n1 - 4c \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( c \\), we get:\n\n\\[\n1 \\geq 4c \\implies c \\leq \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of the function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\n\\]",
